SUMMARY:Experimental/Behavioural – Ralph Bayer (Adelaide) – Selling en 
 bloc with vote buying - experimental tests of different rules.
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3053
DESCRIPTION:Ralph Bayer: “Selling en bloc with vote buying - experimental
  tests of different rules.”\nhttps://researchers.adelaide.edu.au/profile
 /ralph.bayer\n\nZoom: https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/82654401351?pwd=V6TMN2ZpNR
 ILVKO2Y5za5S7GjfbAGG.1&from=addon\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps.app.goo.gl/
 BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nDevelopers who buy up individual flats in 
 strata-titled buildings with the aim to redevelop face the exposure proble
 m. This causes otherwise profitable and socially efficient redevelopments 
 not to occur. The resulting holdup-problem can be overcome by en-bloc sale
 s where the whole building is sold as one. The mechanism by which unit own
 ers collectively decide to sell is crucial for allocative efficiency. An u
 nanimity rule where all unit owners must agree to sell ensures that any sa
 le is pareto improving. Unfortunately\, many otherwise efficient sales wil
 l not occur. As a reaction\, some Australian States followed the lead of S
 ingapore and introduced laws that allow en-bloc sales with qualifying majo
 rities of 75 percent. While this mitigates the problem of efficient sales 
 being blocked\, now inefficient sales might occur\, and over-all efficienc
 y is still not guaranteed.\n\n \n\nWe propose an alternative voting mechan
 ism where owners can buy votes for or against a sale and the proceeds are 
 redistributed to all the owners (based on ideas of Weyl\, Posner\, and Lal
 ley). In large homogeneous elections\, in equilibrium voters cast votes fo
 r or against a proposal proportional to their personal gain or loss from t
 he implementation of the proposal if the cost for votes is quadratic\, whi
 ch implies efficiency.  In strata sales the number of owners is typically 
 small and owners and units are heterogenous. Hence\, the efficiency proper
 ties of such a rule are unclear. We generate an experimental environment o
 f strata sales with two treatments\, quadratic cost vs. linear cost of vot
 es and compare the outcome to the hypothetical results generated by a stan
 dard one-vote 75 percent rule.  We find that both the vote-buying mechanis
 ms significantly improve efficiency. The fraction of efficient outcomes ri
 ses from 54 percent to 78 percent with linear cost and to 81 percent with 
 quadratic cost.
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/82654401351?pwd=V6TMN2ZpNRILVKO2Y5za5S7GjfbAG
 G.1&from=addon
END:VEVENT
