SUMMARY:PhD Brown Bag Seminar – Maxine Zhang (University of Manchester) 
 – Hard Information Disclosure and Product Conflation
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3065
DESCRIPTION:Maxine Zhang: “Hard Information Disclosure and Product Confla
 tion”\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTR
 ACT:\nThis paper studies a market in which the monopolist decides whether 
 to disclose hard information about the product for sale to heterogeneous b
 uyers. If the seller withholds information about several products\, buyers
  are unable to distinguish among them\, a situation referred to as product
  conflation. We investigate why the seller conflates products and which pr
 oducts are subject to conflation. Our main findings show that among all pu
 re-strategy equilibria\, the seller’s expected revenue weakly increases 
 with the number of products conflated. Therefore\, the full disclosure equ
 ilibrium\, proven to always exist\, yields the lowest equilibrium revenue 
 in the game. Conflation equilibria arise only when consumer preferences ar
 e sufficiently heterogeneous. Under a class of single-peaked binary prefer
 ences\, this condition is met when individual peaks are more dispersed. Th
 ese results provide a theoretical explanation for the widespread practice 
 of incomplete product information disclosure in markets and show that mand
 atory disclosure policies may hurt welfare.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f874e8c198d10a8c6400851fee88f8ee@generated
DTSTAMP:20261006T002638Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261006T002638Z
SEQUENCE:0
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20261009T141000
DTEND;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20261009T145000
SUMMARY:PhD Brown Bag Seminar – Charon Yang (University of Melbourne) –
  Information Sharing\, Platform Design\, and Price Competition: Evidence f
 rom Retail Gasoline
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3066
DESCRIPTION:Charon Yang: “Information Sharing\, Platform Design\, and Pri
 ce Competition: Evidence from Retail Gasoline”\n\nGoogle Maps: https://m
 aps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nThis paper examines how eas
 ier access to real-time price information affects competition in retail ga
 soline markets. We exploit the two-stage rollout of FuelCheck in New South
  Wales: a website in 2016 and a mobile app in 2017 that made the same pric
 e information substantially easier to use. Comparing 130 NSW markets with 
 65 markets in neighbouring Victoria\, we find little evidence that the web
 site affected retail margins. In contrast\, the app launch is associated w
 ith a 1.7 cents-per-litre reduction in NSW margins relative to Victoria\, 
 with a larger short-run decline of around 4 cents. We find little correspo
 nding change in price dispersion. The results suggest that the competitive
  effects of price transparency depend not only on whether information is a
 vailable\, but also on how easily consumers can access and use it.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d081dc23d0ab63ccb0150278adc12a3c@generated
DTSTAMP:20261006T002638Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261006T002638Z
SEQUENCE:0
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20261009T145000
DTEND;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20261009T153000
SUMMARY:PhD Brown Bag Seminar – Anthony Van Den Berg (University of Melbo
 urne) – Goals and Visibility
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3067
DESCRIPTION:Anthony Van Den Berg: “Goals and Visibility”\n\nGoogle Maps
 : https://maps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nHow do autonomy 
 and visibility shape the effectiveness of goals? I develop a model combini
 ng reference-dependent preferences and image concerns\, in which a goal si
 gnals the agent's ambition. When baseline performance varies little across
  agents\, the model predicts that a uniform externally set goal outperform
 s self-set goals in aggregate\, but this ordering reverses when performanc
 e varies widely. Moreover\, this switching point depends on visibility. Wh
 en social image concerns are strong enough\, there exists some intermediat
 e level of variation such that externally set goals outperform in private\
 , but self-set goals outperform when goals and outcomes are visible. I wil
 l test these predictions in a laboratory experiment using two rounds of Ra
 ven's Matrices and varying the goal designs across agents.
END:VEVENT
