SUMMARY:PhD Brown Bag Seminar – Emily Tran (University of Melbourne) – 
 Gender Differences in Professional Self-Presentation on LinkedIn: Evidence
  from Australia
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3069
DESCRIPTION:Emily Tran: “Gender Differences in Professional Self-Presenta
 tion on LinkedIn: Evidence from Australia”\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps.
 app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nGender differences in professio
 nal self-presentation may contribute to gender gaps in how workers are eva
 luated and\, ultimately\, to broader inequalities in the labor market. Thi
 s paper examines whether men and women differ in how they describe themsel
 ves and their work on LinkedIn. Using large-scale profile data from Reveli
 o Labs on individuals currently employed in Australia\, we compare men and
  women in similar occupations and at similar levels of seniority. We analy
 ze language in profile headlines\, About sections\, and job descriptions\,
  focusing on three dimensions of professional self-presentation: agentic v
 ersus communal language\, individual versus collective credit claiming\, a
 nd the use of quantitative language to describe responsibilities and accom
 plishments. We also examine whether gender differences in these measures p
 ersist within narrowly defined occupational groups and how they vary acros
 s industries with different levels of female representation. As the projec
 t is at an early stage\, the presentation will focus on the motivation\, m
 easurement approach\, and proposed empirical strategy.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:8df69a87a31a971780e9f93b4fc492a9@generated
DTSTAMP:20261006T002638Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261006T002638Z
SEQUENCE:0
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20261016T145000
DTEND;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20261016T153000
SUMMARY:PhD Brown Bag Seminar – Gloria Chen (University of Melbourne) –
  Sorting Talent\, Distorting Skills: How Precision Shapes Meritocratic Sel
 ection?
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3070
DESCRIPTION:Gloria Chen: “Sorting Talent\, Distorting Skills: How Precisi
 on Shapes Meritocratic Selection?”\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps.app.goo.
 gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nSelection mechanisms such as exams aim 
 to sort talent into high-return positions but also shape applicants’ ski
 ll investment. Exams capture only a subset of productive skills\, encourag
 ing over-investment in tested skills and under-investment in others. I stu
 dy how noise in exam scores affects skill composition and realised product
 ivity after allocation. I find that realising the benefits of an exam depe
 nds critically on its precision: even when an exam can deliver higher prod
 uctivity than a lottery\, poorly chosen precision can undermine its benefi
 ts. This occurs because\, starting from a precise exam\, adding noise rapi
 dly weakens sorting gains before it substantially reduces skill distortion
 s. At intermediate noise levels\, productivity losses can therefore outwei
 gh the remaining sorting gains. Further increasing noise makes allocation 
 sufficiently unresponsive to investment\, prompting a more balanced skill 
 composition. The reduction in productivity losses can allow sorting gains 
 to dominate again. Improving a poorly designed exam may therefore require 
 either greater precision to strengthen sorting or greater noise to reduce 
 skill distortions.
END:VEVENT
